meta {
  name: Login
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}/auth/login
  body: json
  auth: none
}

body:json {
  {
    "username": "{{username}}",
    "email": "{{email}}",
    "password": "{{password}}"
  }
}

vars:pre-request {
  email: mbathepaul@gmail.com
  password: @#Fa22raptor
  username: mouchard
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.status === 200 && res.body?.access_token) {
    bru.setEnvVar("access_token", res.body.access_token);
    bru.setEnvVar("refresh_token", res.body.refresh_token || "");
    bru.setEnvVar("user_id", res.body.user_id || "");
  }
}

docs {
  Exchanges credentials for a JWT. Response is captured into env vars (`access_token`, `refresh_token`, `user_id`) so every following request uses `Authorization: Bearer {{access_token}}` automatically.

  The body sends BOTH `username` and `email`. The backend accepts either:
  - if `username` is non-empty, it looks up by external_id = username
  - else if `email` is non-empty, it looks up by email OR external_id = email
  - else returns "Username or email is required"

  So you only need ONE of the two vars to be set to a real account value. The other can stay as an empty env var — the server ignores empty fields.

  **Troubleshooting** — if you get "Username or email is required" even though vars are set: `vars:pre-request` doesn't always substitute into body:json depending on Bruno version. Canonical fix: edit `environments/Local.bru` and put your real credentials there (replace `admin@digitorn.local` + `changeme-12345`).
}
